room prop_13
kind geometry
import Newton, Principia, Andrew Motte trans., 1729 (Wikisource); Book I, Section III, Proposition XIII; Plate 5, Figure 3.
caption If a body moves in the perimeter of a parabola, find the law of the centripetal force tending to the focus.
final 4
ceiling prop13 :: Qv^2 = 4PS\cdot Pv \qquad F \propto \dfrac{1}{SP^2}

station 1
  gloss A body moves on a parabola with force directed to the focus S.
  color parabblue #1E6FE0
  color fociorange #E8770A
  color radgreen #00A35A
  panel
    point D1 @(-5.0,-5.0)
    point D2 @(-5.0,5.0)
    point S @(-3.0,0.0) color=fociorange heart marker=dot label=$S$ at=SW
    point P @(2.0,4.6) marker=dot label=$P$ at=NE
    parabola_fd par focus S directrix D1 D2 color=parabblue
    segment SP S P color=radgreen heart label=$SP$ at=N
  text
    Newton has proven the inverse-square law for the {parabblue|ellipse}. Now he treats \
    the {parabblue|parabola} — the limiting orbit that opens to infinity. A body at $P$ \
    feels a force drawn to the {fociorange|focus $S$}, and {radgreen|$SP$} is the radius \
    joining them. The question: does this open orbit still yield $F \propto 1/SP^2$? \
    Newton answers yes, by the same Prop. VI machinery.

station 2
  gloss From the next place Q draw QR parallel to SP, QT perpendicular, and Qv parallel to the tangent.
  color parabblue #1E6FE0
  color constpurple #8E24AA
  panel
    point D1 @(-5.0,-5.0)
    point D2 @(-5.0,5.0)
    point S @(-3.0,0.0)
    point P @(2.0,4.6) marker=dot label=$P$ at=NE
    point G @(2.0,-4.6)
    point Q @(1.3,3.9) marker=dot label=$Q$ at=NW
    point v @(1.55,4.2) marker=dot label=$v$ at=S
    parabola_fd par focus S directrix D1 D2 color=parabblue
    segment SP S P
    segment PG P G
    tangent_at tan on par at P color=constpurple heart
    parallel QR through Q to S P color=constpurple label=$QR$ at=NE
    perp QT through Q to S P color=constpurple label=$QT$ at=E
    parallel Qv through Q to P G color=constpurple label=$Qv$ at=SW
  text
    Retaining the preceding lemma's construction, let $Q$ be the place into which the body \
    next succeeds. The {constpurple|standard Prop. VI apparatus} appears: draw $QR$ parallel \
    to $SP$ and $QT$ perpendicular to $SP$. Then add the parabola-specific ordinate — \
    {constpurple|$Qv$ parallel to the tangent}, meeting the diameter $PG$ at $v$ and the \
    distance $SP$ at $x$. These lines let us measure the deflection $QR$ against the geometry \
    of the curve.

station 3
  gloss Similar triangles give QR = Pv, and the conic property gives Qv squared = 4PS times QR.
  color parabblue #1E6FE0
  color relgreen #00A35A
  panel
    point D1 @(-5.0,-5.0)
    point D2 @(-5.0,5.0)
    point S @(-3.0,0.0)
    point P @(2.0,4.6) marker=dot label=$P$ at=NE
    point M @(-3.0,4.6)
    point v @(1.55,4.2) marker=dot label=$v$ at=S
    point x @(1.05,3.5) marker=dot label=$x$ at=SW
    parabola_fd par focus S directrix D1 D2 color=parabblue
    segment SP S P
    segment SM S M
    segment Pv P v color=relgreen label=$Pv$ at=E
    segment Px P x color=relgreen heart label=$QR=Pv$ at=SE
    segment xv x v
    polygon tri1 P x v color=relgreen
    polygon tri2 S P M
  text
    Because triangles $Pxv$ and $SPM$ are similar, and the parabola's focal property makes \
    the sides $SP$ and $SM$ equal, the corresponding sides $Px$ (that is $QR$) and $Pv$ are \
    also equal — {relgreen|$QR = Pv$}. Now invoke the conic sections: the square of the \
    ordinate equals the rectangle of the latus rectum and $Pv$. By Lemma XIII the parabola's \
    latus rectum is $4PS$, so {relgreen|$Qv^2 = 4PS \cdot Pv = 4PS \cdot QR$}. The curve's \
    geometry has collapsed cleanly into the focal distance.

station 4
  gloss As Q meets P the ratio Qv to Qx tends to unity, yielding force reciprocal to SP squared.
  color parabblue #1E6FE0
  color resultred #D81B60
  panel
    point D1 @(-5.0,-5.0)
    point D2 @(-5.0,5.0)
    point S @(-3.0,0.0) marker=dot label=$S$ at=SW
    point N @(-3.0,4.6)
    point P @(2.0,4.6) marker=dot label=$P$ at=NE
    point Q @(1.3,3.9) marker=dot label=$Q$ at=NW
    point T @(0.55,2.4) marker=dot label=$T$ at=S
    point x @(1.05,3.5) marker=dot label=$x$ at=SW
    parabola_fd par focus S directrix D1 D2 color=parabblue
    segment SP S P
    segment SN S N
    segment QT Q T
    segment Qx Q x color=resultred label=$Qx$ at=N
    polygon triQ Q x T color=resultred
    polygon triS S P N
    circle_cr foc S radiusval 0.14 color=resultred heart
  text
    As $P$ and $Q$ coincide, the ratio of $Qv$ to $Qx$ becomes a ratio of equality by \
    Cor. 2 of Lemma VII, so $Qx^2 \to 4PS \cdot QR$. From the similar triangles $QxT$ and \
    $SPN$, $Qx^2 : QT^2 = PS^2 : SN^2 = PS : SA$, whence {resultred|$QT^2 \propto SP \cdot QR$}. \
    By Cor. 1 of Prop. VI the force is measured as $1/(SP^2 \cdot QT^2/QR)$, and substituting \
    gives the {resultred|inverse-square law $F \propto 1/SP^2$}. The parabola obeys the same \
    law as the ellipse. \textit{Q.E.D.}
